    "created_at": "2018-03-15T21:33:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-03-15T21:33:33Z",
    "body": "I understand the desire to see more support behind parsing monitoring specifically, but there's already interest and energy going into monitoring failures and forcing increased strictness in all layers of a web page load.  I have a hard time believing that devs and monitoring products want control and remote detection of any type of failure _except_ invalid syntax.\r\n\r\nCurrent/upcoming landscape:\r\n- [NEL](https://www.w3.org/TR/network-error-logging/) - report various network failures\r\n- [HPKP](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7469) - block, report certificate failures - dead now, but it was way more complex/risky to use than parsing failure handling\r\n- Parsing errors - ???\r\n- [CSP](https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP/) - block, report security failures\r\n- [X-XSS-Protection](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/X-XSS-Protection) - block, report XSS\r\n- [JavaScript errors](https://www.google.com/search?q=log+javascript+errors+to+server) and \"use strict\" - handle most errors that happens after parsing\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
