    "created_at": "2018-03-16T02:41:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-03-16T02:41:51Z",
    "body": "What is the parsing standard the human or automated review uses to verify that the error reported by the browser is accurate? For example, given \r\n\r\n`<p><div>div element in HTML markup as child of p element</div></p>`\r\n\r\nwould dispatch what meaningful message to server, \r\n\r\n`\"div is invalid child element of p element\"`\r\n\r\n? Does such a mapping exist for all possible element parent-child element relationships, or would one need to be created and maintained? \r\n\r\nWhat should occur for custom elements registered at the `document` after page load, or custom elements used within HTML without being registered at all?\r\n\r\nWhat are the specific rules used for parsing error report generation and human or automated correction? \r\n\r\nIf data is sent to a server, how is that data validated and verified as being correct? \r\n\r\nIf the data is validated and verified at server after serving the data, the data can be tested before serving the data.\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
