    "created_at": "2018-03-16T03:12:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-03-16T03:12:23Z",
    "body": "> Does such a mapping exist for all possible element parent-child element relationships, or would one need to be created and maintained?\r\n\r\nI'm not expecting full schema or semantic validation - that's probably not practical due to perf/complexity concerns, though it would be nice to have.  Definitely not asking for browsers to just merge in an HTML/XML validator.  I'm just aiming for visibility into syntax errors, ideally everything under [Parse errors](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#parse-errors).  Anecdotally, parse errors are the more common cause of catastrophic page failures and are more likely to indicate an XSS vulnerability.\r\n\r\n> If data is sent to a server, how is that data validated and verified as being correct?\r\n\r\nSame issue with anything else using the Reporting API - you're at the mercy of client implementations, and you have to assume that any information being sent to this endpoint is untrusted user input.\r\n\r\n> If the data is validated and verified at server after serving the data, the data can be tested before serving the data.\r\n\r\nAgain, this doesn't cover all cases.  You have problematic plugins, proxies/CDNs, service workers, caches, browser bugs, etc. that can and do result in markup modification.  \"It looks good on the server's end\" just doesn't work in the real world.  That's part of the reason the Reporting API exists, to get visibility from the client's perspective.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
